COMMAND NAME: gpaddpart

Adds new child table partitions to a parent table that was 
previously partitioned using gpcreatepart.


*****************************************************
SYNOPSIS
*****************************************************

gpaddpart -t <parent_table> -n <new_child_count> [-c] [-q] [-a] 
[-l <log_dir>] [-D] [-d <master_data_directory>] [-x <database>]

gpaddpart -t <parent_table> -s <new_start_value> [-c] [-q] [-a] 
[-l <log_dir>] [-D] [-d <master_data_directory>] [-x <database>]

gpaddpart -t <parent_table> -o <new_list_value>[,<value>,...] [-c] 
[-q] [-a] [-l <log_dir>] [-D] [-d <master_data_directory>] [-x <database>]

gpaddpart -? | -v



*****************************************************
DESCRIPTION
*****************************************************

The gpaddpart script adds additional child tables to a parent table 
that has already been partitioned using gpcreatepart. This is useful 
if you need to create additional child table partitions for newly added 
data. It will use the same partitioning scheme (date range, numerical 
range, or list) as was originally defined when the parent table was 
first partitioned. It performs the following tasks:

* Creates new child tables that inherit all attributes from the parent 
  table (columns, constraints, distribution key, etc.), as well as the 
  original partitioning attributes. Note that permissions are not inherited. 
 
* Defines CHECK constraints on the child tables based on the new date 
  range, numeric range, or list value specified. The child table names 
  are created using a predefined naming convention of:
  <top_level_parent>_child_depth_<#>_<partition_type>_<data_range>_<type>

* If the existing child tables have rewrite rules, it will create the 
  appropriate rewrite rules on the parent table so that INSERT statements 
  are automatically redirected to the correct child table. The rewrite 
  rules are created using a predefined naming convention of:
  rule_<child_table_name>

* If the existing child tables have indexes, it will create the 
  corresponding indexes on the new child tables. Indexes are created 
  with a predefined naming convention of:
  idx_<column_number>_<child_table_name>

* If the parent table contains data, it moves partitioned records 
  from the parent table and inserts them into the correct child table 
  based on the CHECK constraints.


*****************************************************
OPTIONS
*****************************************************

-t <parent_table>

Required. The name of the parent table that for which you want to 
create new partitioned child tables.


-s <new_start_value>

Required for numeric range partitioning. Specifies the numeric value 
to use as the starting point for creating additional child tables.


-n <new_children_count>

Required for date range partitioning. Specifies the number of additional 
month, day, or hour child tables to create from where the partitioning 
scheme last left off.


-o <new_list_values>

Required for list partitioning. A comma separated list of values for 
which to create new list partitioned child tables. Make sure there are 
no spaces between list values and comma separators. To add a null check 
constraint add the text NULL to the list.


-c (leave data in parent table)

Copies the newly partitioned records back into the parent table after 
the child tables have been created. The default behavior is to remove 
records from the parent table and put them into the correct child table.


-q (no output)

Do not output messages to the screen. Write messages to log file only.


-a (do not prompt)

Do not prompt the user for confirmation.


-l <log_directory>

Specifies an alternative directory to write the log file. By default 
log files are written to ~/gpAdminLogs.


-D (debug)

Sets log output to debug level.


-x database_name

Optional. The name of the database where the parent table resides. If 
not supplied, the script uses the default database (which is the most 
recently created database).


-d <master_data_directory>

Optional. The master data directory. If not specified, the value set for 
$MASTER_DATA_DIRECTORY will be used.


-? (help)

Displays the online help.


-v (show script version)

Displays the version, status, last updated date, and check sum of this script.


*****************************************************
EXAMPLES
*****************************************************

Add new partitioned child tables to a parent table that is currently 
range partitioned by salary. You want to increase the top salary 
range from what it currently is to $1,000,000.

gpaddpart -t employee -s 10000000


Add new partitioned child tables to a parent table that is currently 
list partitioned by region. You want to add a new region for china and india.

gpaddpart -t sales -o china,india


Add two new partitioned child tables to a parent table that is 
currently date partitioned by month.

gpaddpart -t sales -n 2


